BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Living Declaration: When in the Course of Human Events
DTSTART:20260930T000000
DTEND:20260930T000000
DTSTAMP:20260930T172235Z
UID:a286c46b25c979837d00d0c900d07c@events-near-me
DESCRIPTION:Why do we have a Declaration of Independence? Why does it incl
 ude language about human rights? In conversation with MHS Chief Historian 
 Kanisorn Wongsrichanalai\, historian and former presidential speechwriter 
 Ted Widmer ( The Living Declaration ) explores the complex lineage of the 
 words and ideas in the Declaration and the dramatic sequence of events\, m
 any of them originating in Massachusetts\, that led to the creation of the
  most consequential state paper in history. Join us for a lively discussio
 n and the opportunity to view key MHS holdings like Thomas Jefferson's ori
 ginal manuscript copy of the Declaration. Featuring Ted Widmer\, historian
  and former presidential speechwriter\, in conversation with MHS Chief His
 torian Kanisorn Wongsrichanalai. This is a hybrid event in partnership wit
 h Library of America. The in-person reception starts at 5:00 and the progr
 am will begin 6:00 PM. Sources: Meet Boston Event page: https://www.meetbo
 ston.com/event/the-living-declaration%3a-when-in-the-course-of-human-event
 s/93393/\n\nEvents Near Me: within 30 miles of Northborough\, MA 01532\nDi
 scovery: Local fallback from massachusetts-events.ics\nSource page: https:
 //www.meetboston.com/event/the-living-declaration%3a-when-in-the-course-of
 -human-events/93393/
LOCATION:Massachusetts Historical Society\, 1154 Boylston Street\, Boston
 \, MA\, 02215
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-living-declaration%3a-when-in-the
 -course-of-human-events/93393/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Way We Never Were
DTSTART:20260930T000000
DTEND:20260930T000000
DTSTAMP:20260930T172235Z
UID:9637b3f02a95949d521affeafa5b0a@events-near-me
DESCRIPTION:To celebrate our new exhibition The Way We Never Were: The Ame
 rican Professional Photographers Collection \, join us for a roundtable co
 nversation reflecting on Barbara Norfleet’s expansive influence on photo
 graphy at Harvard as a teacher\, curator\, and artist. Kiku Adatto\, Peter
  Galassi\, Lauren Greenfield\, and Susan Meiselas will share personal memo
 ries and talk about how Norfleet helped shape the study and practice of ph
 otography across generations. Following the lecture\, guests are invited t
 o visit the exhibition on Level 3. How to join: Register here. Fee: This i
 s a free event. Parking: Limited free parking available at Broadway Garage
 \, 7 Felton Street. Where to go: Enter on Broadway side of the building an
 d head to Menschel Hall. Accessibility accommodations: If you would like t
 o request accommodations for your visit\, please contact Visitor Services 
 at am_register@harvard.edu or 617-495-9400. For more details about accommo
 dations at the museums\, please see the Accessibility page. Speakers: Kiku
  Adatto\, Scholar-in-Residence at the Mahindra Humanities Center and Lectu
 rer on Social Studies\, Harvard University Peter Galassi\, Writer\, curato
 r\, and art historian Lauren Greenfield\, Artist\, documentary photographe
 r\, and documentary filmmaker Susan Meiselas\, Artist Audrey Sands\, Richa
 rd L. Menschel Associate Curator of Photography\, Harvard Art Museums Sour
 ces: Meet Boston Event page: https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-way-we-n
 ever-were/93384/\n\nEvents Near Me: within 30 miles of Northborough\, MA 0
 1532\nDiscovery: Local fallback from massachusetts-events.ics\nSource page
 : https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-way-we-never-were/93384/
LOCATION:Harvard Art Museums\, 32 Quincy Street\, Cambridge\, MA\, 02138
URL:https://www.meetboston.com/event/the-way-we-never-were/93384/
END:VEVENT
